\fs96 \cf2 Intro to pointers\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 A pointer is an indirect reference to one or more values stored in memory. The pointer is a value that holds an address to memory, and provides APIs to store and retrieve values to that memory. The value pointed to by a pointer is also known as a pointee.\
The Mojo standard library includes several types of pointers, which provide different sets of features. All of these pointer types are generic\'97they can point to any type of value, and the value type is specified as a parameter. For example, the following code creates an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 OwnedPointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  that points to an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 from\cf14  std\cf5 .\cf14 memory \cf5 import\cf14  OwnedPointer\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  ptr\cf5 :\cf14  OwnedPointer\cf5 [\cf14 Int\cf5 ]\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13 ptr \cf5 =\cf14  OwnedPointer\cf5 (\cf15 100\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ptr
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  variable has a value of type 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 OwnedPointer[Int]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . The pointer points to a value of type 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , as shown in Figure 1.\
