\fs27\fsmilli13600 \cf2 Table 1. Pointer types\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf2 \super 1
\fs32 \nosupersub  
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 OwnedPointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ArcPointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  implicitly allocate memory when you initialize the pointer with a value.\

\fs26\fsmilli13333 \super 2
\fs32 \nosupersub  
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 UnsafePointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  provides unsafe methods for initializing and destroying instances of the stored type. The user is responsible for managing the lifecycle of stored values.\

\fs26\fsmilli13333 \super 3
\fs32 \nosupersub  
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 OwnedPointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is explicitly copyable, but explicitly copying an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 OwnedPointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  copies the stored value into a new 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 OwnedPointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
The following sections provide more details on each pointer type.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs56 \cf10 \cb12 Pointer
\fs64 \cf2 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/memory/pointer/Pointer/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 Pointer}} type is a safe pointer that points to a initialized value that it doesn't own. Some example use cases for a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Pointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  include:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Storing a reference to a related type. For example, a list's iterator object might hold a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Pointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  back to the original list.\
\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Passing the memory location for a single value to external code via 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 external_call()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Where you need an API to return a long-lived reference to a value. (Currently the iterators for standard library collection types like 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 List
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  return pointers.)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 You can construct a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Pointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to an existing value by calling the constructor with the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 to
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  keyword argument:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 from\cf14  std\cf5 .\cf14 memory \cf5 import\cf14  Pointer\cb1 \
\
\cb13 ptr \cf5 =\cf14  Pointer\cf5 (\cf14 to\cf5 =\cf14 some_value\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 You can also create a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Pointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  by copying an existing 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Pointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
A 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Pointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  carries an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/values/lifetimes/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 origin}} for the stored value, so Mojo can track the lifetime of the referenced value.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs56 \cf10 \cb12 OwnedPointer
\fs64 \cf2 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/memory/owned_pointer/OwnedPointer/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 OwnedPointer}} type is a smart pointer designed for cases where there is single ownership of the underlying data. An 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 OwnedPointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  points to a single item, which is passed in when you initialize the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 OwnedPointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 OwnedPointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  allocates memory and moves or copies the value into the reserved memory.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 from\cf14  std\cf5 .\cf14 memory \cf5 import\cf14  OwnedPointer\cb1 \
\
\cb13 o_ptr \cf5 =\cf14  OwnedPointer\cf5 (\cf14 some_big_struct\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 An owned pointer can hold almost any type of item, but when constructing an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 OwnedPointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , the stored item must be either 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Movable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  or 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Copyable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
Since an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 OwnedPointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is designed to enforce single ownership, the pointer itself can be moved, but not copied.\
Note: Currently, you can't create an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Optional[OwnedPointer[T]]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  because the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Optional
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  type only works with types that are both movable and copyable. This restricts some use cases that would otherwise be a natural fit for 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 OwnedPointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , including self-referential data structures like linked lists and trees. (Until this use case is supported for 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 OwnedPointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , it's possible to use 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ArcPointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  where you need a smart pointer that can be 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Optional
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs56 \cf10 \cb12 ArcPointer
\fs64 \cf2 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 An {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/memory/arc_pointer/ArcPointer/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 ArcPointer}} is a reference-counted smart pointer, ideal for shared resources where the last owner for a given value may not be clear. Like an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 OwnedPointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , it points to a single value, and it allocates memory when you initialize the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ArcPointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  with a value:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 from\cf14  std\cf5 .\cf14 memory \cf5 import\cf14  ArcPointer\cb1 \
\
\cb13 attributesDict\cf5 :\cf14  Dict\cf5 [\cf14 String\cf5 ,\cf14  String\cf5 ]\cf14  \cf5 =\cf14  \cf5 \{\}\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13 attributes \cf5 =\cf14  ArcPointer\cf5 (\cf14 attributesDict\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
