\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Note: The reference count is stored using an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/os/atomic/Atomic/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 Atomic}} value to ensure that updates to the reference count are thread-safe. However, Mojo doesn't currently enforce exclusive access across thread boundaries, so it's possible to form race conditions.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 UnsafePointer\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/memory/unsafe_pointer/UnsafePointer/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 UnsafePointer}}
\fs32  is a low-level pointer that can access a block of contiguous memory locations, which might be uninitialized. It's analogous to a raw pointer in the C and C++ programming languages. 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 UnsafePointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  provides unsafe methods for initializing and destroying stored values, as well as for accessing the values once they're initialized.\
As the name suggests, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 UnsafePointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  doesn't provide any memory safety guarantees, so you should reserve it for cases when none of the other pointer types will do the job. Here are some use cases where you might want to use an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 UnsafePointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 :\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Building a high-performance array-like structure, such as 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 List
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  or 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Tensor
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . A single 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 UnsafePointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  can access many values, and gives you a lot of control over how you allocate, use, and deallocate memory. Being able to access uninitialized memory means that you can preallocate a block of memory, and initialize values incrementally as they are added to the collection.\
\ls7\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Interacting with external libraries including C++ and Python. You can use
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 UnsafePointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to pass a buffer full of data to or from an external library.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 For more information, see {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/pointers/unsafe-pointers/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 Unsafe pointers}}.\
